  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Why do skyscrapers sway?",
      "a": "Wind gusts and earthquakes push tall buildings sideways, and like a ruler twanged on a desk, a tall building springs back and forth at its own natural rhythm.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do tall buildings move?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a tuned mass damper?",
      "a": "It is a large mass, often hundreds of tonnes, mounted near the top of a tall structure on springs or cables and tuned to the building's natural rhythm. As the building sways, the mass moves against it and soaks up the motion.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the giant ball in Taipei 101?",
        "How does a damper stop swaying?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why must the damper be tuned?",
      "a": "The mass only cancels sway well if it swings at the same natural rhythm as the building. Engineers set its cable length or spring stiffness so the two match.",
      "asks": [
        "What does tuned mean in a tuned mass damper?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What do the shock absorbers do?",
      "a": "Hydraulic shock absorbers between the mass and the building resist its movement and turn the energy of the sway into heat, so the motion dies away instead of carrying on.",
      "asks": [
        "Where does the swaying energy go?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Does a damper make a building stronger?",
      "a": "Not really; the building is already strong enough to be safe. The damper mainly reduces the swaying people feel, making upper floors more comfortable in strong winds.",
      "asks": [
        "Is a tuned mass damper for safety or comfort?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The tuned mass damper in Taipei 101 is a steel sphere weighing about 660 tonnes, hung between the 87th and 92nd floors.",
    "Tuned mass dampers are also fitted to some bridges, chimneys and tall towers."
  ],
